<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Royal and Parliamentary Titles Act 1927</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Royal and Parliamentary Titles Act 1927 was an act of the UK Parliament that changed the formal name of the state to the United <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain</a> and Northern Ireland, recognising the separation of the Irish Free State in 1922. It also allowed the royal style and titles to be altered accordingly by proclamation.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-irish-treaty">Anglo-Irish Treaty</a> most of Ireland left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland</a> in December 1922 to become the Irish Free State, while six north-eastern counties in Ulster remained as Northern Ireland. The King's title, proclaimed under the Royal Titles Act 1901, still named him King of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland and of the British Dominions beyond the Seas. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-conference">Imperial Conference</a> of 1926 agreed that this hardly fitted the new position of the Free State as a Dominion. As the royal style could only be changed by the Parliament at Westminster, the British government introduced a bill in March 1927, which passed both Houses easily and received royal assent on 12 April 1927.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The act had three main provisions. It allowed the King to change the royal style and titles by proclamation within six months, without itself setting out the new form, as earlier acts had done. It renamed the Westminster parliament the Parliament of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland. And it laid down that in every later act and public document United Kingdom would mean Great Britain and Northern Ireland, unless the context required otherwise.</p><p>The proclamation followed on 13 May 1927 in the London Gazette. It replaced Britanniarum in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> style with Magnae Britanniae, Hiberniae, and in the English style made George V King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, Ireland and the British Dominions beyond the Seas. A new Great Seal of the Realm and Great Seal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, designed by Percy Metcalfe, were delivered to the Privy Council on 27 October 1930.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The style changed again in 1948, when Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> was dropped after the independence of India. On the accession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a> in 1952 each Commonwealth realm began to pass its own legislation on the royal style, the United Kingdom by the Royal Titles Act 1953, and only in May 1953 was Ireland in the royal title changed to Northern Ireland.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Royal and Parliamentary Titles Act 1927” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Royal and Parliamentary Titles Act</b> of 1927 changed the formal name to the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom">United Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The remaining state was formally renamed the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland by the <b>Royal and Parliamentary Titles Act</b> of 1927.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-great-britain-and-ireland">United Kingdom of Great Britain and Ireland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
